WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.172
Permanent updates should follow a safe workflow.

00:00:03.172 --> 00:00:08.297
Start with a select query that identifies exactly which rows would change.

00:00:08.297 --> 00:00:10.577
Then begin a transaction.

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:18.797
Next, perform the update and use the returning feature so Postgres immediately shows the rows affected by the change.

00:00:18.797 --> 00:00:20.931
Inspect the results carefully.

00:00:21.427 --> 00:00:25.609
If everything is correct, commit the transaction and keep the changes.

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:30.119
If something is wrong, roll back the transaction and discard them.

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:34.459
Think of a transaction as a protected testing area for your update.

00:00:34.459 --> 00:00:39.339
This is especially important when you are learning, working with production data,

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:42.399
or applying a transformation to many rows.

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:50.859
A simple habit can prevent a serious mistake: select first, update inside a transaction, inspect the results,

00:00:50.859 --> 00:00:53.004
and only then decide whether to commit.
